INT. NIGHT-SHIFT CAR WASH - 3 A.M.

Fluorescent lights. Wet concrete. A conveyor hums, empty.

Four people around a break-room table inside a glass booth. Vending coffee. Donuts.

NADIA SZABO (30s, manager) counts the till. DOMINIC "DOM" ACHEBE (20s) eats. OLD HARLAN PRICE (70s) does a crossword. LUIS FERRO (50s) stirs his coffee for too long.

DOM
Seven letters, "cheerful and quiet."

HARLAN
Serene.

NADIA
That's six.

LUIS
(not looking up)
Nobody's cheerful at three in the morning.

DOM
I'm cheerful.

NADIA
You're asleep.

HARLAN
Placid. Seven.

DOM
He's eating.

NADIA
Both.

Quiet. The conveyor hums. Headlights sweep the glass.

A black sedan rolls to the entrance and stops. Nobody gets out.

NADIA (CONT'D)
Closed sign's on.

HARLAN
Sign's always on.

LUIS
(low)
Nobody go out.

DOM
Why not?

LUIS
That's the third night. Same car. Same plate. Look at the plate.

They look. Dom stops chewing.

HARLAN
Who parks and waits at a car wash?

LUIS
A man who's already clean.

NADIA
Luis...

The sedan's door opens. A polished shoe touches wet concrete. MR. VANCE (50s, camel coat, pleasant face) steps out and walks toward the booth, hands visible, smiling.

LUIS
(whispering)
He asked me last night where the cameras are.

NADIA
And you said?

LUIS
Nothing.

HARLAN
Good.

DOM
Then why's he coming to ask again?

Nadia's hand slides under the counter and closes on something cold.
